# Later content

A script runs as the document is parsed, so it sees what comes before it. Two functions give content that needs more: the whole document, or the whole workspace.

## After the document {#post-parse}

`nml.post_parse(callback)` returns a placeholder; once the whole document is parsed, it becomes what `callback` returns, in the same Lua state. A summary before what it sums up is the typical use:

:example
````
:lua
```
HEADINGS = {}
nml.on("heading", function(h)
  table.insert(HEADINGS, h.text)
end)
```

Headings from here on:
:lua`nml.post_parse(function()
  return table.concat(HEADINGS, ", ")
end)`.
````

## When the page is linked {#defer}

`nml.defer(module, name, args)` returns a placeholder that becomes what the function `name` of the imported `module` returns when the page is *linked*: after every page is parsed, and again in later builds when what it reads changes. The function runs in a new Lua state, with `nml.pages()`: the workspace's pages, with their titles, URLs and exported variables.

:example
```
The workspace has :lua`nml.defer("docs.demo", "count")` pages.
```

The count follows pages as they are added and removed, without parsing this page again. [[std#nav|std.nav]] makes its menu this way. `args` must be data — numbers, strings, booleans, tables of them — as the call is stored with the page.

| `nml.pages()` field | |
|:--------------------|:-|
| `path` | The page's file, in the workspace. |
| `name` | Its name in references. |
| `title` | Its title. |
| `url` | Its URL, relative to the page being linked. |
| `current` | Whether it is the page being linked. |
| `variables` | The variables it [[variables#export|exports]], as written. |

`nml.tasks()` lists the [[tasks#|tasks]] of the workspace's pages; the page is linked again when they change:

| `nml.tasks()` field | |
|:--------------------|:-|
| `title` | Its text. |
| `state` | A heading's keyword (`TODO`, …), or an item's checkbox: `todo`, `partial`, `done`. |
| `done` | Whether it is done. |
| `heading` | Whether it is a heading, rather than a list item. |
| `date`, `scheduled`, `deadline` | Its timestamps: `YYYY-MM-DD`, or `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM` with a time; `nil` without one. |
| `page` | Its page, as `nml.pages()` shows it. |

## What they can return {#limits}

Content made after parsing cannot hold what needs parsing: references, numbered figures and tables, anchors, rendered math and graphs, and `nml.parse`. Text, links, styles, lists and the other elements are fine.
